1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 e6 3. g3 Nf6 4. d4 dxc4 5. Bg2 c5 6. O-O Nc6 7. Qa4  (7. dxc5 {often in the catalan accepted, after c5 you just want to take and play the resulting endgame, black still has issues with his light ssquared bishop. }  Qxd1 8. Rxd1 Bxc5 9. Nbd2 Ng4 10. Ne4 Be7 11. h3 Nf6 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. Nd2 Na5 14. Ne4 Be7 15. Bf4 Bd7 16. b4 cxb3 17. axb3 Nxb3 18. Rab1 Na5 19. Nd6+ Bxd6 20. Bxd6 Bc6 21. Bxc6+ bxc6 22. Bc5)  (7. Ne5  Nxd4  (7... Nd5  )  (7... Bd7  )) Bd7 8. Qxc4 b5 9. Qxb5?? {completely missing Bb5 which I saw instantly after the move, this is a case where sitting on my hands would have been best and expanding my candidate moves and not holding bias. }  (9. Qc3! {[%cal Gc5d4]Qc3 was best, keeping an advantage and the central tension. and also threatening to take on c5 if black doesn't force the issue.}  Nd5  (9... Ne4  10. Qc2)  (9... Rc8  10. dxc5) 10. Qd2) Nxd4 10. Qd3 Bb5 11. Qe3 Nd5 12. Qd2 Nxe2+ 13. Kh1 Nxc1 14. Rxc1 Be7 {[%cal Gf3e5,Gb1c3]} 15. Na3  (15. Nc3! {nc3 here was best, aiming to trade of black's good knight for white's bad knight, this also adds pressure to the g2 a8 diagonal,  and forcing the issue as it forks the pieces. }  ) Bc6 16. Ne5 Bb7 17. Rd1 O-O 18. Nac4 Qc7 19. Rac1 Rfd8 {[%cal Gd2a5]} 20. Qe2?! {I wanted to keep winning chances on by keeping the queens on, but sometimes the best way to keep fighting is to trade to a better endgame. }  (20. Qa5! {I did actually consider this. If black takes then white's knights get into the position and allow counterplay and white is back to equal }  Qxa5 21. Nxa5 Ba6 22. Nac6 Bf8  (22... Re8  23. Nxe7+ Rxe7 {winning the bishop pair back would move it back to equality})) Rac8 21. h4?? {[%cal Ge7h4,Gd5f4]completely missing bxh4 and nf4 tactics} Bf6 22. Kg1 g6 23. Bh3  (23. h5 {this was my intuition. my intuition normally is right, give it much more weight in games}  ) Re8 24. Ng4 Bg7 25. Nge3 Nxe3 26. Nxe3 Red8 27. h5 Qe5 28. hxg6 hxg6 29. Qb5 Rxd1+ 30. Rxd1 Bf3 31. Rd2 Rb8 32. Qa4 Rxb2 33. Rx